; ======================================================================
;  dsi_panel_v2_dcs.pi4 - the 202-entry DCS initialisation table the
;  second-generation Waveshare 10.1 inch panel needs before it will show
;  anything, and the prepare sequence around it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE FROM dsi_panel_v2.pi4. That file is the
;  MCU at I2C $45 - the rails, the resets and the backlight, all of it
;  over a two-wire bus that works with no DSI link at all. This file is
;  the PANEL, and every byte in it goes out over the DSI link in escape
;  mode. They are two different buses to two different parts, and the
;  first one working tells you nothing about the second.
;
;  It is also why the bench could be in the exact state it was left in
;  on 2026-09-06: the MCU answering, the rails up, the backlight
;  commanded on, and the glass showing nothing. Nothing had ever been
;  said to the panel itself.
;
;  ---- USE ------------------------------------------------------------
;
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/i2c.pi4"
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dsi_panel_v2.pi4"
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dsi_host.pi4"
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dsi_panel_v2_dcs.pi4"
;
;    DsiV2Begin()          ; the MCU, over I2C
;    ...DsiHostBringUp()   ; the link, so escape mode exists
;    DsiPanelPrepare()     ; rails, the panel's own reset, then the table
;
;  House rule 10: a library never includes a library. This one calls
;  into three others and the main file lists all of them ahead of it.
;
;  ---- WHERE THE TABLE COMES FROM (do not guess - rule 21) ------------
;
;  RaspberryPi4/Reference/rpi-6.12.y_panel-waveshare-dsi-v2.c, lines
;  356-560: ws_panel_10_1_a_init[], which is the init member of
;  ws_panel_10_1_inch_a_desc (:2156-2163) - the descriptor the
;  "10_1_inch_a" overlay string selects (:2288), which is the string the
;  bench board's own overlay line carries.
;
;  IT WAS EXTRACTED MECHANICALLY, NOT TYPED. Two hundred entries
;  transcribed by eye would contain a mistake and there would be no way
;  to find it: a wrong gamma byte does not fail, it just makes the
;  picture subtly wrong for ever. The bytes below were generated from
;  the C source by a script that refuses any line it cannot parse, so
;  either every entry is right or nothing was emitted.
;
;  ---- THE ENCODING, AND WHY IT IS A BYTE STREAM ----------------------
;
;  There is no array of structures to put in a DataSection here, so the
;  table is a stream of variable-length records read by one loop:
;
;      $01 cmd            a DCS command with no parameter
;      $02 cmd param      a DCS command with one parameter
;      $FE ms             wait that many milliseconds
;      $FF                the end
;
;  The count byte comes FIRST so the reader never has to look ahead to
;  know how far to step. The two markers are $FE and $FF because no DCS
;  command byte in THIS table is $FE or $FF - which is a property of
;  this table, checked by the generator, and not a property of DCS. A
;  table that grew such a command would need an escape and this loop
;  would need to be told about it.
;
;  ---- WHAT THE LAST FOUR ENTRIES ARE ---------------------------------
;
;  The table ends with $11 (Sleep Out), 120 ms, $29 (Display On), 60 ms.
;  Those two commands and those two delays are the standard MIPI DCS
;  wake-up and they are IN the table rather than after it, so the whole
;  sequence is one thing that either ran or did not. Everything before
;  them is this panel's own controller configuration - page selects at
;  $E0, then gamma, then the gate and source mapping.
; ======================================================================

EnableExplicit

; The two record markers above.
#DSI_INIT_END   = $FF
#DSI_INIT_DELAY = $FE

; Rail and reset delays, from ws_panel_prepare
; (panel-waveshare-dsi-v2.c:1743-1771): iovcc then 20 ms, avdd then
; 20 ms, reset low 60 ms, reset high 60 ms. dsi_panel_v2.pi4 carries the
; same measured numbers for its own half; they are stated separately
; here because a constant defined from another constant is refused by
; this compiler, and both statements cite the same source line.
#DSI_PANEL_MS_RAIL  = 20
#DSI_PANEL_MS_RESET = 60

; The three expander bits this file drives, by the device-tree property
; that names each one (rpi-6.12.y_vc4-kms-dsi-waveshare-panel-v2-overlay.dts).
;   :50  reset-gpio = <&display_mcu 1>   the PANEL's reset
;   :51  iovcc-gpio = <&display_mcu 4>
;   :52  avdd-gpio  = <&display_mcu 0>
; BIT 1 IS THE PANEL AND BIT 9 IS THE TOUCH CONTROLLER. They live one
; apart in the same 16-bit shadow, and writing the wrong one leaves a
; perfectly good panel dark while the touch controller quietly reboots.
#DSI_PANEL_BIT_AVDD  = 0
#DSI_PANEL_BIT_RESET = 1
#DSI_PANEL_BIT_IOVCC = 4

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HOW MANY FAILURES IN A ROW BEFORE THE TABLE GIVES UP.
;
;  Not one - the whole point of running on past a failure is to tell
;  "the link carries nothing" from "one command in the middle failed",
;  and stopping at the first throws that away.
;
;  But not two hundred either. On 2026-09-07 every command failed, and
;  because each one waited out its own timeout the board answered
;  nothing - not the console, not a ping - for a hundred seconds. It had
;  not hung; there was simply no way to tell from outside.
;
;  Five in a row is enough to have established which of the two worlds
;  this is, and short enough that the console comes back while somebody
;  is still watching.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DSI_PANEL_GIVE_UP = 5

Global dsi_panel_sent.i   = 0    ; DCS commands that completed
Global dsi_panel_failed.i = 0    ; and how many did not
Global dsi_panel_failat.i = -1   ; the index of the first that did not
Global dsi_panel_gaveup.i = 0    ; 1 if it stopped early, on a run

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiPanelInitTable() - walk the stream and send it.
;
;  IT DOES NOT STOP AT THE FIRST FAILURE, and that is deliberate on a
;  bring-up. Linux gives up on the first error (:1731-1737) because a
;  half-initialised panel is no use to a running system. The question
;  being answered here is whether this link carries commands at all, and
;  "the first one failed and so did the other 199" is a completely
;  different answer from "one in the middle failed". The index of the
;  first failure is kept so the two cannot be confused.
;
;  Returns the number of commands that completed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiPanelInitTable()
  Define i.i
  Define b.i
  Define idx.i
  Define run.i

  dsi_panel_sent   = 0
  dsi_panel_failed = 0
  dsi_panel_failat = -1
  dsi_panel_gaveup = 0

  i   = 0
  idx = 0
  run = 0
  Repeat
    b = PeekA(?dsi_panel_v2_init + i) & $FF

    If b = #DSI_INIT_END
      Break
    EndIf

    If b = #DSI_INIT_DELAY
      delay(PeekA(?dsi_panel_v2_init + i + 1) & $FF)
      i = i + 2
      Continue
    EndIf

    If b = 1
      If DsiDcsWrite(PeekA(?dsi_panel_v2_init + i + 1) & $FF, 0, 0) = 0
        dsi_panel_failed = dsi_panel_failed + 1
        run = run + 1
        If dsi_panel_failat < 0
          dsi_panel_failat = idx
        EndIf
      Else
        dsi_panel_sent = dsi_panel_sent + 1
        run = 0
      EndIf
      i = i + 2
      idx = idx + 1
      If run >= #DSI_PANEL_GIVE_UP
        dsi_panel_gaveup = 1
        Break
      EndIf
      Continue
    EndIf

    If b = 2
      If DsiDcsWrite(PeekA(?dsi_panel_v2_init + i + 1) & $FF, PeekA(?dsi_panel_v2_init + i + 2) & $FF, 1) = 0
        dsi_panel_failed = dsi_panel_failed + 1
        run = run + 1
        If dsi_panel_failat < 0
          dsi_panel_failat = idx
        EndIf
      Else
        dsi_panel_sent = dsi_panel_sent + 1
        run = 0
      EndIf
      i = i + 3
      idx = idx + 1
      If run >= #DSI_PANEL_GIVE_UP
        dsi_panel_gaveup = 1
        Break
      EndIf
      Continue
    EndIf

    ; A record length that is not 1, 2, $FE or $FF means the stream has
    ; been mis-stepped, and carrying on would send whatever byte came
    ; next as if it were a command. Stop rather than transmit noise.
    dsi_panel_failed = dsi_panel_failed + 1
    Break
  ForEver

  ProcedureReturn dsi_panel_sent
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiPanelPrepare() - ws_panel_prepare (:1743-1771): the rails, the
;  panel's own reset, and then the table.
;
;  THE CALLER OWNS THE I2C BUS. This selects nothing and restores
;  nothing, because the display bus has to be selected before it is
;  called and the caller is the only one that knows what to put back.
;  Same contract touch_cmd.pi4 already uses.
;
;  Returns 1 only if every expander write ACKed AND every DCS command
;  completed. A 0 with DsiPanelSent() at zero is a link that carried
;  nothing; a 0 with the expander failing is a panel that was never
;  powered, and those are not the same fault.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiPanelRails()
  Define ok.i

  ok = 1

  If DsiV2Pin(#DSI_PANEL_BIT_IOVCC, 1) = 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  delay(#DSI_PANEL_MS_RAIL)

  If DsiV2Pin(#DSI_PANEL_BIT_AVDD, 1) = 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  delay(#DSI_PANEL_MS_RAIL)

  If DsiV2Pin(#DSI_PANEL_BIT_RESET, 0) = 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  delay(#DSI_PANEL_MS_RESET)

  If DsiV2Pin(#DSI_PANEL_BIT_RESET, 1) = 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  delay(#DSI_PANEL_MS_RESET)

  ProcedureReturn ok
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiPanelPrepare() - the rails, then the table.
;
;  THE TWO HALVES ARE SEPARATE PROCEDURES because the order between them
;  and the DSI host's own bring-up is a question this bench has not
;  answered yet. A powered panel holds its lanes at a defined level; an
;  unpowered one does not, and a host brought up against undriven lanes
;  latches a contention error before anything has been asked of it.
;  Being able to run the rails FIRST, without sending a single command,
;  is what makes that testable.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiPanelPrepare()
  Define ok.i

  ok = DsiPanelRails()

  DsiPanelInitTable()
  If dsi_panel_failed <> 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn ok
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DsiPanelSent()   : ProcedureReturn dsi_panel_sent   : EndProcedure
Procedure.i DsiPanelFailed() : ProcedureReturn dsi_panel_failed : EndProcedure
Procedure.i DsiPanelFailAt() : ProcedureReturn dsi_panel_failat : EndProcedure
Procedure.i DsiPanelGaveUp() : ProcedureReturn dsi_panel_gaveup : EndProcedure

